The patch trurlic carries on the tree-sitter runtime, vendored in vendor/tree-sitter.

Upstream: the crate tree-sitter 0.27.2 as published on crates.io (the binding crate and the
C runtime it compiles, from tree-sitter's `lib/` at commit
3fb7be49145acfb07e33b61ed5689e2b6771b2e9), MIT, its LICENSE kept. Vendored as published,
less what a native build reads nothing of: Cargo.lock, Cargo.toml.orig,
.cargo_vcs_info.json, and src/wasm-stdlib but its imports.txt (the libc the wasm32 target
compiles). Every other file is upstream's, unchanged but by this patch.

What the patch changes, in src/alloc.{c,h}, src/parser.c and src/stack.{c,h}, with no change
to the API or to any tree a parse gives:
1. A step of a walk of the parse stack, and a round of the reductions error recovery tries,
   are progress operations, which the progress callback sees: the callback is called once
   per 100 operations crossed, so the count of its calls is a parse's work divided by 100.
2. The default allocator counts the bytes it gives each thread, and every 64 of them is one
   progress operation, so a count of operations bounds what a parse allocates.
3. stack_node_release and stack_node_add_link run over arrays on the heap in place of
   recursion, in the recursion's order, so a parse takes a fixed call stack whatever its
   input.

To update the runtime: vendor the new release the same way, apply this patch from
vendor/tree-sitter (`patch -p1 < ../tree-sitter-0.27.2-bounds.patch`), resolve what no longer
applies, regenerate this patch, and raise code::INDEX_FORMAT.

